.TH ASK 1 "October 2026" "cli-ask 0.3.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ask \- non-intrusive terminal AI assistant with session-scoped context and chatbot mode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPROMPT\fR]
.br
.B ask
\fB--chatbot\fR | \fB-i\fR [\fIPROMPT\fR]
.br
.B ask
[\fI--no-context\fR]
.br
.B ask
\fB--\fR \fIPROMPT\fR
.br
.I COMMAND
|
.B ask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPROMPT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ask
is a lightweight, zero-dependency command-line utility designed for system administrators, DevOps engineers, and terminal users who want immediate, fluff-free AI answers without disrupting their shell workflow.
.PP
Unlike bloated TUI-based AI assistants, \fBask\fR acts as a native Unix filter: it executes directly from standard command pipelines, streams concise answers directly to standard output, and exits immediately. AI responses are formatted in terminal-native plain text with ASCII underlines and clean indentation, avoiding unrendered web markdown.
.PP
When invoked with \fB--chatbot\fR or \fB-i\fR, \fBask\fR launches an interactive continuous REPL session, allowing natural conversational back-and-forth prompting within the terminal without exiting after each turn.
.PP
When invoked with no prompt arguments in an interactive terminal, \fBask\fR enters multiline input mode, accepting literal text, code snippets, or command chains until terminated by standard OS EOF (\fBCtrl+D\fR on Linux/macOS, \fBCtrl+Z\fR then Enter on Windows).
.PP
Conversational context is automatically preserved and scoped to the parent shell process ID (PPID). Follow-up questions in the same terminal window retain knowledge of prior turns without cross-contaminating other shell tabs or tmux panes. Long conversations are automatically compacted to maintain responsiveness and avoid token window exhaustion.
.PP
When outputting to an interactive terminal, a subtle horizontal splitter line separates user queries from AI responses. On non-terminal stdout or redirection pipelines, status indicators and dividers are automatically suppressed to preserve clean data streaming.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-chatbot " [" \fIPROMPT\fR "]"
Start a continuous interactive chat session (REPL). If an initial prompt is provided, it is submitted upon launch.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-clear
Clear the conversation history for the current terminal session.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-context
Execute the query as a standalone request without loading or updating the session history.
.TP
.B \-\-update\-self ", " \-\-update
Check for the latest release on GitHub and update the running binary in-place.
.TP
.B \-\-
POSIX end-of-options delimiter. All following arguments are treated strictly as literal prompt text, ignoring any dashes or flags.
.TP
.BR \-\-set\-key " " \fIAPI_KEY\fR
Save your OpenRouter API key to the user configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-\-set\-model " " \fIMODEL\fR
Set the default OpenRouter model slug (defaults to \fBnvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-config
Display the current configuration file path, active model, session identifier, and masked API key.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print program version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage help and exit.

.SH CHATBOT COMMANDS
Within interactive chatbot mode (\fBask -i\fR), the following commands are available at the \fB>>>\fR prompt:
.TP
.BR /clear ", " /c
Clear conversation memory for the current session.
.TP
.BR /model " [" \fIMODEL\fR "]"
Switch the active model for the chat session, or display current model if no argument is given.
.TP
.B /help
Display available chatbot commands.
.TP
.BR exit ", " quit ", " /q
Exit chat mode.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENROUTER_API_KEY
If set, overrides the API key saved in the configuration file.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ask/config.json
User configuration containing API credentials and preferred model.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ask/sessions/session\-<PPID>.json
Session-scoped conversation history for the active terminal shell.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Quick command lookup:
ask "how to find listening ports in linux"
.TP
.B Continuous chatbot session:
ask -i
.TP
.B Chatbot with initial question:
ask -i "explain how systemd cgroups work"
.TP
.B Passing literal command arguments:
ask ps -eo pid,cmd --sort
.TP
.B Interactive multiline entry:
ask
.TP
.B Follow-up in same terminal:
ask "filter only tcp ones"
.TP
.B Self-updating to latest version:
ask --update-self
.TP
.B Standard input piping:
git diff | ask "summarize these changes"
.TP
.B Clear active session:
ask --clear

